1. Folder Structure Example:

📦 What each package usually means:

model (or domain)

Contains data classes / entities that represent your business objects.

In your Portfolio Manager project → these would be classes like:

Product

Electronics, Clothing, Grocery

Position, Portfolio, Trade

Category (enum)

👉 Think of it as: “the nouns of your system.”

service

Contains the business logic (the operations you perform on your models).

In your project → classes like:

CatalogManager (manages products)

PortfolioManager (calculates P&L, manages trades/positions)

TradeService (executes buy/sell operations)

👉 Think of it as: “the verbs / actions of your system.”

util (utilities / helpers)

Contains helper classes and reusable functions that don’t belong directly to model or service.

Examples:

DateUtil (formatting dates for reports)

MathUtil (financial formulas, rounding P&L)

LoggerUtil (common logging helpers)

👉 Think of it as: “the toolbox for shared functionality.”

src/main/java/com/yourname/portfoliomanager/
    model/
        Product.java
        Electronics.java
        Category.java
        Position.java
        Portfolio.java

    service/
        CatalogManager.java
        PortfolioManager.java
        TradeService.java

    util/
        DateUtil.java
        MathUtil.java

✅ Why do this?

Keeps your code organized and modular.

Easier for other developers (or future you) to navigate.

Follows industry best practices.

👉 In short:
They’re asking you to separate your data (model), your logic (service), and your helpers (util) instead of dumping everything in one package.]

